      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s funny, \u0027cause it was Gemini that suggested `::` to begin with, claiming it was impossible in URLs. But when thinking of it a bit more, it could appear in IPv6 addresses. (I am not sure if your example is valid or not, as I am not 100% sure if the `::` gets converted behind the scenes to `%something`. But I don\u0027t think it matters; either example disqualifies `::`.)\n\nWhen looking at `|`, I am worried about it being misleading, as it mean something to the shell, too.\n\nI think a better solution is to reverse the order to `file::url`, and ignore the theoretical issue. When it comes to URLs, we record things outside our control or awareness; but for the path on local disk, the rare user who happens to use a path with `::` can just rename their file or directory.",
